/-! ## §6.1 Case $J$: $\frac12\le x\le y\le 1$ -/

/-
This is the most critical case. In particular, this is where the precise values of $\beta_0$,
$c_0$ originate.
Recall from Corollary \ref{cor:JsatisfiesBobkovtwopt} [3.5] that for $x_0\le x\le y\le 1$ the
function $J$ satisfies the best possible estimate, $G^1_{\beta}[J](x,y)\ge 0$ for
$\beta=\frac12$. When $x<x_0\approx 0.552$ this estimate fails for $\beta=\frac12$.
-/

/-- [DIR], §6.1 (cf. Remark 3.6): when $x<x_0\approx0.552$ the estimate
$G^1_{\frac12}[J](x,y)\ge0$ fails: for every $x\in[\frac12,x_0)$ there is $y\in[x,1]$ with
$G^1_{\frac12}[J](x,y)<0$. -/
theorem caseJ_G₁_half_fails (x : ℝ) (hx : x ∈ Ico (1 / 2) x₀) :
    ∃ y ∈ Icc x 1, G₁ (1 / 2) J x y < 0 := by
  sorry

/-- **Proposition 6.1** ([DIR]). Let $\beta\ge\beta_0=0.50057$ and $c_0=0.997$. Then for
$\frac12\le x\le x_0$ and $x\le y\le1$,
$$G^1_\beta[J](x,y)\ge0\quad\text{and}\quad G^1_{\frac12}[c_0J](x,y)\ge0.$$ -/
theorem prop_6_1 (β : ℝ) (hβ : β₀ ≤ β) (x y : ℝ) (hx : 1 / 2 ≤ x) (hx₀ : x ≤ x₀)
    (hxy : x ≤ y) (hy : y ≤ 1) :
    G₁ β J x y ≥ 0 ∧ G₁ (1 / 2) (c₀ • J) x y ≥ 0 := by
  sorry

/-- [DIR], §6.1, after Proposition 6.1: by monotonicity in $\beta$, we only need to show
$G^1_{\beta_0}[J]\ge0$ to show the first part: for $\beta\ge\beta_0$ and
$\frac12\le x\le y\le1$, $G^1_{\beta_0}[J](x,y)\le G^1_\beta[J](x,y)$. -/
theorem caseJ_G₁_mono_β (β : ℝ) (hβ : β₀ ≤ β) (x y : ℝ) (hx : 1 / 2 ≤ x) (hxy : x ≤ y)
    (hy : y ≤ 1) : G₁ β₀ J x y ≤ G₁ β J x y := by
  sorry

